\section{Why a parser needs a core}

A parser is a function from text to meaning, but the function that does the
real work --- the one that matches a token, that repeats a rule, that tries
an alternative --- does not look like a function from text to meaning.  It
looks like a function from a cursor to a maybe-value.  Three questions have
to be answered on every call, and a parser library has to answer them in a
way that composes.

The first question is \emph{where am I}.  Every parser has to read a
character, sometimes look ahead one character, and sometimes report which
characters it just swallowed.  The natural representation is a mutable
cursor over an immutable buffer.

The second question is \emph{did it work}.  A parser either produces a
value or it does not, and the ``does not'' case has to carry enough
information for the caller to decide what to do next: retry a different
alternative, or give up and tell the user what was expected.

The third question is the hard one: \emph{what happens when a parser
consumes some input and then fails}.  A backtracking parser must be able
to undo the consumption; a parser that has committed to a path must be able
to say so, so that the alternatives are not tried.  Without a way to
distinguish a recoverable failure from an unrecoverable one, ordered choice
degenerates into exponential search, and error messages become a lottery.

DSLtk's answer is small and explicit.  The header implements the whole
parser core in about a hundred and thirty lines: a cursor
(\code{ParsecInput}), a two-valued failure kind (\code{ParseFailureKind}),
an error record (\code{ParseError}), a result type that carries either a
value or an error (\code{ExpectedResult<T>}), a parser value
(\code{Parser<T>}), and four free functions that glue them together
(\code{parser}, \code{merge_error}, \code{fail_expected} and
\code{load_parse_input_file}).  Everything else in this part of the book
is built out of those.

Two design decisions deserve to be stated up front, because they explain
almost every observable behaviour later in the chapter.  First, the parser
is a \emph{value}: \code{Parser<T>} is a thin wrapper around a
\code{std::function}, so parsers are copied, stored in containers, and
passed around like any other object.  Second, the parser is
\emph{stateless}: all mutable state lives in the \code{ParsecInput} that the
caller passes by non-const reference.  A parser can therefore be run
concurrently on two inputs, and the same parser object can be reused for a
million inputs without any hidden bookkeeping.

\section{A worked example}

The core is small enough to use directly, without any combinator at all.
The following program implements a parser for one fixed literal token by
hand, and reports where the mismatch occurred.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A hand-written parser for a fixed token, and a diagnostic printer for the error it produces.},label={lst:ch21-token}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// A hand-written parser for a fixed literal token.  It is *not* built
// from dsl::ch: it drives the cursor itself, which is exactly what a
// primitive parser in the combinator library does.
//
// Note the discipline: the parser rewinds the cursor to where it started
// when it fails, but it reports the failure at the offset where the
// mismatch actually occurred.  The two positions are independent.
static dsl::Parser<std::string>
token (std::string lit)
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [lit] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>
      {
        std::size_t start = in.pos;
        for (char c : lit)
          {
            if (in.eof () || in.peek () != c)
              {
                std::size_t bad = in.pos;
                in.pos = start;
                return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>::failure (
                    bad, dsl::ParseFailureKind::Soft,
                    { "token '" + lit + "'" });
              }
            in.consume ();
          }
        return lit;
      });
}

// A tiny diagnostic printer.  The header has no message formatter; this
// is the user's job.
static std::string
format_expected (const std::vector<std::string> &labels)
{
  if (labels.empty ())
    return "<nothing>";
  std::string s;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < labels.size (); ++i)
    {
      if (i)
        s += (i + 1 == labels.size () ? " or " : ", ");
      s += labels[i];
    }
  return s;
}

static void
report (const std::string &src)
{
  auto outcome = dsl::run_parser (token ("let"), src);
  std::cout << "  source: \"" << src << "\"\n";
  if (outcome.value)
    {
      std::cout << "    matched \"" << *outcome.value << "\"\n";
      return;
    }
  const auto &err = outcome.error;
  std::cout << "    error at offset " << err.pos << " ("
            << (err.kind == dsl::ParseFailureKind::Committed ? "committed"
                                                            : "soft")
            << "): expected " << format_expected (err.expected) << "\n";
  std::cout << "    ";
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < src.size (); ++i)
    std::cout << ' ';
  std::cout << std::string (err.pos, ' ') << "^\n";
}

int
main ()
{
  report ("let");
  report ("let x = 1;");
  report ("lst");
  report ("le");
  report ("");
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

Compiling against \code{DSLtk/DSLtk.hpp} and running it produces:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch21-token}.},label={lst:ch21-token-out}]
  source: "let"
    matched "let"
  source: "let x = 1;"
    error at offset 3 (committed): expected <end-of-input>
                 ^
  source: "lst"
    error at offset 1 (soft): expected token 'let'
        ^
  source: "le"
    error at offset 2 (soft): expected token 'let'
        ^
  source: ""
    error at offset 0 (soft): expected token 'let'
    ^
\end{lstlisting}

Three things in that output are worth pausing over.  The second case is not
a failure of the token parser at all: the token matched, and the error
comes from \code{run_parser}, which refuses to leave trailing input
behind; that is the \code{Committed} error labelled
\code{"<end-of-input>"} discussed in Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-diagnostics}.
The third and fourth cases show the distinction the hand-written parser
makes between the two positions: the cursor is rewound to offset zero, but
the error is reported at offset one and offset two respectively, because
that is where the input stopped matching the token.  The fifth case shows
the same thing at the end of the input.

