It is just as important to record what the core does \emph{not} have.
\code{ParsecInput} has no \code{position()}, no \code{remaining()}, no
\code{skip()} and no \code{expect()}; the cursor is a bare offset into a
view, and everything else is derived from it.  It records no error state
of its own: a failure lives in the \code{ExpectedResult} that the parser
returns, never in the input.  \code{ExpectedResult<T>} has no
\code{operator->}, so a success must be reached with \code{(*r).field} or
\code{r.value->field}; it has no \code{value_or()}, no
\code{map()} and no \code{error_or()}.  Its \code{operator bool} is
\emph{explicit}, so a result cannot be converted to \code{bool} by
accident, and \code{operator*} on a failed result dereferences an empty
\code{std::optional}, which is undefined behaviour --- the caller must
test first.

The \code{ParseError} record deserves one more word, because it is the
single most consequential design decision in the core.  A \code{ParseError}
does not know its line or its column; it knows a byte offset into the
source.  Line and column are properties of the \emph{file}, and a parser
that is handed a fragment of a file --- a string view into the middle of a
buffer --- has no way to recover them.  Computing them is therefore the
user's job, and Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-diagnostics} shows a formatter that
does it.

\begin{invariant}[The cursor is the only mutable state]
\label{inv:ch21-cursor}
Between two calls to a parser, the only state that survives is the
\code{pos} member of the \code{ParsecInput}.  A parser that wants to
remember anything between calls must put it in the value it returns, or
capture it in the closure that implements it.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Why the result is not a variant}

The most surprising thing about \code{ExpectedResult<T>} is that it is not
a \code{std::variant}.  The obvious design --- a variant of \code{T} and
\code{ParseError} --- would halve the size of the result and would make
``did it work'' a single index test.  The shipped design keeps two
independent members instead, and the reason is that the two pieces of
information are genuinely independent.

A \code{ParseError} is not an alternative to a value; it is
\emph{metadata about the attempt}.  A successful parse still has an
attempt, and the attempt has a position at which it succeeded, a kind
(which is vacuously \code{Soft}), and an empty list of labels.  A failed
parse still has a value slot, which is empty.  Keeping the two apart
means the error can be carried along and merged --- which is exactly what
\api{merge_error} does --- without any of the ceremony that a variant
would impose on every access.  The price is that the type is bigger than
it needs to be, and that a successful result carries a meaningless error
that a careless caller might read.

There is a second, more practical reason.  \code{ExpectedResult<T>} is
constructed in a great many places, and the type must be usable in a
\code{return} statement from a lambda whose return type is deduced.  With
a variant, every such construction would have to name the alternative
explicitly.  With two members and a set of constructors, the code reads
as ordinary value semantics: \code{ExpectedResult<T>::failure(pos, kind,
expected)} builds a failure, and the implicit conversion from \code{T}
builds a success.

The result is also \emph{not} a \code{std::expected}, and this is worth
noting because the two are easy to confuse.  \code{std::expected} keeps
the error in the same alternative as the value, so a success and a failure
are mutually exclusive by construction.  \code{ExpectedResult} keeps them
apart, and the \code{operator bool} reports only on the value slot.  A
failed result is \code{false}, and that is the only question
\code{operator bool} answers.

\subsection{The parser as a value}

\code{Parser<T>} holds one member, a
\code{std::function<ExpectedResult<T>(ParsecInput&)>}, and forwards
calls to it.  Three consequences follow, and all three are used in the next
chapter.

First, a parser is \emph{copyable} and therefore storable.  A grammar can
be built once, at start-up, and then run against a million inputs; the
combinators of Chapter~\ref{ch:primitive-parsers} capture their operands
by value precisely so that this works.

Second, a parser is \emph{inspectable and replaceable}.  The
\code{parse} member is public, so a parser can be taken apart and put
back together.  This is not a stylistic accident: it is the only way to
write a recursive grammar with combinators that capture by value, and
Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} shows the technique.

Third, a parser is \emph{stateless with respect to the input}.  Two
threads may run the same parser on two different inputs simultaneously,
because the only mutable state is the \code{ParsecInput} each thread
supplies.  The parser object itself is not modified by a call.

The deduction in \api{parser} deserves a word.  The callable's return
type must be an \codel{ExpectedResult<}{Something>}, and the
\code{value_type} member of that result is used to deduce \code{T}.  The
deduction is not from the callable's argument type --- there is only one
argument type --- but from the nested \code{value_type}, which is why
\code{ExpectedResult<T>} bothers to define one in the first place.  The
practical consequence is that a lambda must state its return type, either
by annotating it or by having an unambiguous \code{return} statement; a
lambda whose body returns two different types will fail to deduce, with
a message that mentions \code{value_type} rather than the lambda.

\section{The algorithm: the commit discipline}

Everything subtle in the parser core follows from one rule:

\begin{contract}[The commit discipline]
\label{contract:ch21-commit}
A parser that consumes input and then fails is responsible for putting
\code{in.pos} back where it was, unless it has decided to commit.  A
failure whose kind is \code{Committed} means \emph{no alternative may be
tried}: the input has been consumed in a way that the caller has promised
not to undo.
\end{contract}

The rule is not enforced by the library; it is a convention that the
library's own combinators follow and that user-written parsers must follow
too.  The convention has two halves, and it is worth keeping them apart.

The first half is the \emph{rewind}.  A parser that fails softly must
leave the cursor exactly where it found it, so that the caller --- typically
\api{operator|} --- can rewind and try something else.  A parser that
fails in a committed way may leave the cursor wherever it likes, because
nobody is going to try anything else.

The second half is the \emph{label}.  The failure record says whether the
failure was soft or committed, and the distinction is what
\api{operator|} uses to decide whether to try the second alternative.
A soft failure is a request: ``this did not match, and you may look
elsewhere''.  A committed failure is a verdict: ``this did not match, and
looking elsewhere would be wrong''.

The \code{ParseFailureKind} enumeration is the whole vocabulary:

\begin{verbatim}
enum class ParseFailureKind { Soft, Committed };
\end{verbatim}

Consider the difference between the two spellings of a keyword parser.  A
keyword parser that matches the letters of \code{if} and then discovers
that the next character is a letter has consumed input.  If it reports a
soft failure and rewinds, the caller may fall back to an identifier
parser.  If it reports a committed failure, the caller must not, and the
error propagates.  The library gives the user the choice, and
\api{try_parse} (Chapter~\ref{ch:primitive-parsers}) is the explicit
operator for choosing the first behaviour.

\subsection{Merging errors}

When two alternatives both fail, the library has to decide which error to
report.  The rule implemented by \api{merge_error} is the familiar
``farthest failure wins'' heuristic, with one refinement.

\begin{pseudocode}[Merge two parse errors]
\textbf{function} merge\_error (dst, src)\\
\quad if src.pos > dst.pos then\\
\qquad dst := src\\
\qquad return\\
\quad if src.pos < dst.pos then\\
\qquad return\\
\quad if src.kind = Committed then\\
\qquad dst.kind := Committed\\
\quad for each label in src.expected do\\
\qquad\quad if label is not in dst.expected then\\
\qquad\qquad\quad append label to dst.expected
\end{pseudocode}

The first two branches implement the heuristic.  The error that was
recorded furthest into the input is the most informative one, because it
is the point at which the parser had committed the most context; an error
at offset zero of a large token is less useful to a user than an error at
the end of it.  When the source error is strictly farther away, it
\emph{replaces} the destination wholesale --- position, kind and labels
alike.  Note the word \emph{replaces}: the kind of the farther error is
adopted, not promoted, so a committed failure at a nearer position can be
discarded in favour of a soft failure at a farther one.  That is
deliberate, and it is the right trade, because the position is what the
user cares about.

The remaining branch handles the tie.  Two alternatives that failed at the
same offset are two answers to the same question --- what would have been
acceptable here? --- so their label lists are unioned, and the result is
promoted to \code{Committed} so that the union is not thrown away by a
further merge.  This is the mechanism behind messages of the form
``expected digit, identifier, or a closing brace''.  The de-duplication is
linear in the length of the two lists, which is fine for the handful of
labels a hand-written grammar produces and noticeable only for a grammar
with thousands of alternatives.

One subtlety is worth flagging, because it is visible in the output of
Listing~\ref{lst:ch21-token} and in the message formatter of
Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-diagnostics}.  The union preserves the order in
which the labels were \emph{appended}, and \api{operator|} merges the
error of the \emph{second} alternative as the destination.  The labels
therefore appear in the reverse of the order in which the alternatives
were written.  A grammar that offers \code{digit} first and \code{'('}
second reports ``expected \code{(} or \code{digit}''.  The set is right;
the order is not the source order, and a formatter that cares should sort
or should be written knowing this.

\subsection{Building a failure by hand}

\api{fail_expected} is the shortest path from ``the cursor is at
position \emph{p} and the byte there is not what I wanted'' to a failed
result.  It records the label at the cursor's \emph{current} position,
which is why a parser that has already consumed input reports the failure
at the end of what it consumed rather than at the start of the attempt.  A
parser that wants to report elsewhere must construct the
\code{ExpectedResult} itself, as the hand-written parser in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch21-token} does; the machinery is public, and
\code{ExpectedResult<T>::failure(pos, kind, expected)} is the direct way
to say exactly where the problem is.

\subsection{Loading input}

\api{load_parse_input_file} is a convenience that reads an entire file
into a \code{std::string} and returns it in a
\code{Result<std::string, std::string>}.  It opens the file in binary
mode, so a file containing a null byte is read faithfully, and it reports
two distinct failures: the file could not be opened, or it could not be
read to the end.  Because the result type is
\code{Result<std::string, std::string>}, both the payload and the error
message are \code{std::string}, and Chapter~\ref{ch:task-pipeline}
explains why that particular instantiation is not inspectable in the
shipped header.

