The figure also shows the three accessors in their natural order.  A
parser almost always begins by asking \code{eof()} or \code{peek()}; it
consumes only when it is sure; and it reports what it consumed with
\code{get_span} when it needs the text rather than a derived value.  The
fourth member, \code{pos}, is public, and the library's combinators write
to it directly --- that is how a rewinding combinator undoes a failed
attempt.  A user-written parser is expected to do the same.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

The core is minimal, and its limits are worth stating precisely.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The source must outlive the parse.}  \code{ParsecInput}
  holds a \code{std::string_view}, not a copy.  A view over a temporary
  is a dangling view: constructing a \code{ParsecInput} directly from a
  temporary \code{std::string} leaves the view referring to a destroyed
  temporary before the next statement runs.  The same hazard applies to
  \code{run_parser}, although there the temporary lives until the end of
  the calling statement, so the call itself is safe.

\item \textbf{A null byte in the source is indistinguishable from the end
  of the source.}  \code{peek()} returns \code{'\textbackslash 0'} at the
  end of input, and it returns the same value for a real null byte.
  \code{eof()} distinguishes the two, and a parser that cares should test
  it.

\item \textbf{\code{consume()} at the end of the input is a no-op.}  It
  returns \code{'\textbackslash 0'} and leaves \code{pos} unchanged, so
  it is safe to call, but a parser that loops on \code{consume()} without
  testing \code{eof()} will spin forever.

\item \textbf{\code{get_span} can throw.}  It computes
  \code{source.substr(start, pos - start)}.  If \code{start} is greater
  than \code{pos}, the length underflows to a huge value, which
  \code{substr} clamps, so the result is a short view rather than a
  disaster; but if \code{start} is beyond the end of the source,
  \code{substr} throws \code{std::out_of_range}.  The argument must be
  an offset that the cursor has already passed.

\item \textbf{A successful result still carries an error.}  Because
  \code{ExpectedResult<T>} is not a variant, the error member of a
  successful result is a default-constructed \code{ParseError}: offset
  zero, kind \code{Soft}, empty label list.  Code that inspects
  \code{r.error} without first checking the result will read a
  meaningless error.

\item \textbf{\code{operator*} on a failed result is undefined behaviour.}
  It dereferences an empty \code{std::optional}.  The explicit
  \code{operator bool} exists to make the test easy, but it does not
  enforce it.

\item \textbf{There is no \code{operator->}.}  A success is reached with
  \code{(*r).field}, \code{r.value->field} or, most often,
  \code{r.value.value_or(...)}.  Code that writes \code{r->field} will
  not compile, and this is a common source of confusion for readers coming
  from \code{std::optional} or \code{std::expected}.

\item \textbf{\code{merge_error} copies whole errors.}  When the source
  error is farther along, the destination is overwritten rather than
  merged, so the labels of the nearer error are discarded.  This is
  intended --- the farther position is the better diagnostic --- but it
  means the union of labels only ever contains labels that failed at the
  same offset.

\item \textbf{The label list is unbounded.}  \code{expected} is a
  \code{std::vector<std::string>} and nothing limits its size.  A parser
  that unions thousands of alternatives produces a correspondingly large
  message, and \code{merge_error} compares labels linearly.

\item \textbf{Errors are positions, not explanations.}  The core does not
  know what a ``token'' is, only which byte it wanted.  Turning an offset
  and a list of labels into a sentence with a line and a column is the
  user's job, and it is done in Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-diagnostics}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The parser core of DSLtk is four small ideas: a cursor that is the only
mutable state, a result that carries its own error, a failure kind that
distinguishes ``try something else'' from ``give up'', and a merge rule
that keeps the most informative error.  Nothing here is clever, and
nothing here is optional: every combinator in the next chapter is a
composition of exactly these pieces.

Chapter~\ref{ch:primitive-parsers} takes the core and adds the
combinators --- and finds that the library's combinator set is far smaller
than one might expect, which makes the commit discipline all the more
important to understand.
